      "question": {
        ":question/id": "bc73c612-2a32-5b85-8989-8b463754a48c",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y$\n- **II.** ${y}^{\u2032}-{e}^{x}y={x}^{2}{y}^{5}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{\u2032}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x=-x{y}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-{e}^{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
