        ":question/id": "e63ce1e7-0f23-5c8c-a5ed-4ae3fc1a265b",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{\u2032}+\\sqrt{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y$\n- **II.** ${y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=x{y}^{3}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{2}y=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
