      "question": {
        ":question/id": "d4b7e54f-2216-5cba-ba81-1d5ec0ebc609",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n- **I.** ${y}^{\u2032}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{3}{y}^{\u2032}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x=\\sqrt{y}$\n- **III.** $y\\,{y}^{\u2032}+x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y={cos}^{2}\u2061y$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}+(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}+x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y}{y})=\\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
